# CP/M-era symbols, as laid out by P25K_B's CBIOS/PBIOS.
#
#   ./p2500-emu --symbols symbols/cpm.sym --disk disks/P25K_B.raw \
#     --max-steps 60000000 --disasm ED2A:12
#
# BUILD-SPECIFIC, and measured as such. Checked against the live RAM of
# every disk here that boots (tools/sym_check.py in the research repo,
# 16-byte windows):
#
#   P25K_B    reference
#   P25K_S    41 of 45 names at the same address, 0 moved
#   P25TEST   41 of 45 names at the same address, 0 moved
#   P25K_G    3 of 45 - do NOT use this file there; see p25k_g.sym
#
# The four that do not compare on S/P25TEST are live data, not refutations:
# TICKS, KBD.RING, GENQ.BUF and DPH.TABLE hold values that differ between
# two runs of the SAME disk, which is the control that says so.
#
# P25K_G carries the older PHILIPS P2000B generation: its SYSCBI.PHI loads
# at $E200 like this one but is a different, shorter build ($E833 vs
# $EABE), so nothing below can be reached by adding an offset. The emulator derives the parts that can be derived - the BDOS entry
# and all 17 CBIOS jump-table slots with their bodies - out of the live
# machine's own page zero, so those are deliberately NOT listed: this file
# is for what a scan cannot see. Source: the research notes' TODO.md,
# "Reference: RAM addresses", CP/M era.
#
# Fingerprint, so `--symbols auto` can recognise this build without
# being told which disk is in the drive:
# $E200-$E5FF is the first kilobyte of this CBIOS build, identical on
# P25K_B, P25K_S and P25TEST and unchanged between runs (it is code,
# not state). Before CP/M is resident that window is empty, so the rule
# cannot match too early.
# match E200:0400:22B19A76
#
# Format: ADDR NAME, hex, one per line. $ or 0x prefix optional, : or =
# accepted in place of the space, # and ; start a comment.

# ---- CP/M itself
CC00 CCP
D400 BDOS.base
E086 BDOS.RESET
E117 BDOS.RDROVEC
E1AD RO.VECTOR
E1AF LOGIN.VECTOR

# ---- console
E274 KEYXLAT.TAB
E34C CAPS.MASK
E34D ESC.COUNT
E482 CAPS.TOGGLE
E4B7 CONIN.XOR
E4F8 CONOUT.XLAT

# ---- disk
E81D DPH.TABLE
E84D DPB
E87C DRIVE.MAP
E880 DRIVE.RECS

# ---- the driver layer
EAC0 REQ.POST
EB56 DEV.TABLE
EBE2 EI.RETI
EBE5 RING.PUSH
EBFB RING.POP

# ---- keyboard
ED02 KBD.RINGINIT
ED2A CTC3.ISR
ED2D CTC3.ISR.body
ED8F KBD.RING

# ---- screen
EDD5 SCREEN.DRV
EDFC SCREEN.FNTAB
EE91 SCREEN.WRITE
F170 ESC.TAB
F199 ESC.TAB2
F43B ATTR.CUR
F454 ATTR.TMPL
F46C CRTC.R10TMPL
F475 GENQ.WPTR
FD23 GENQ.BUF

# ---- clock
F37F CTC2.ISR
F382 CTC2.ISR.body
F436 TICKS

# ---- serial
F546 BAUD.TAB
F597 CTC0.ISR
F5A6 TX.WAIT
F669 CTC1.ISR
F72A TX.FLAGS
F72D TX.TIMEOUT

# ---- CBIOS's own IM2 table, at I=$FF
FF90 CBIOS.IM2

# ---- The driver layer, read out of this build's own device table at
#      $EB56 and each driver's function table. Added when P25K_G's
#      equivalents were derived (tools/port_syms.py --devtable in the
#      research repo), since the same structures name both builds.
EB8B DRV.DISPATCH   ; every driver's `ld hl,fntab / call here`
ECAB KBD.DRV        ; device $30
ECC7 KBD.FNTAB
ED89 KBD.REQ.PTR
F430 SCREEN.REQ.P  
F432 SCREEN.FLAGS   ; bit 2 drives the generated-code queue
F4B9 SER.DRV        ; device $40 - serial/printer; its data sits with
                    ; TX.FLAGS and the baud table
F4D5 SER.FNTAB
F72F SER.REQ.PTR
F7A7 DISK.DRV       ; devices $00-$03
F7D5 DISK.FNTAB     ; nine functions
EC2B DEVFF.DRV      ; device $FF, purpose unidentified
EC40 DEVFF.FNTAB

# ---- The IM2 layer and the rest of the disk structures, same provenance
FEE8 IM2.TRAMP      ; six trampolines, $FEE8 to $FF15, in vector order
FF33 ISR.SHIM       ; saves A, patches its own jp with the handler address
FFE7 ISR.SAVE.HL
FBE8 PIOA.ISR       ; from the trampoline at $FEE8
FB67 DMA.ISR        ; from the trampoline at $FEF1
E809 DRIVE.CUR      ; SELDSK stores the selected drive here
E85C SECTRAN.TAB    ; 01 02 05 06 09 0A 0D 0E ... - CP/M's own translate
E93E DIRBUF         ; from DPH[0]
E905 ALV.A
E8D5 CSV.A
E1C1 BDOS.DPBCOPY   ; BDOS's own copy of the DPB

# ---- Derived straight from this machine's own tables by
#      tools/derive_syms.py (research repo), which needs no
#      reference build: the per-drive check and allocation
#      vectors out of each DPH, and every driver's function
#      entries out of its function table. Function codes are
#      the driver's own; $05 is write on the screen driver.
E8E5 CSV.B
E8F5 CSV.C
E918 ALV.B
E92B ALV.C
EC48 DEVFF.F00
EC83 DEVFF.F03
ECD5 KBD.F00
ED0F KBD.F03
ED1C KBD.F04
EE0D SCREEN.F00
EE3B SCREEN.F01
EE57 SCREEN.F03
EE7D SCREEN.F02
F4E6 SER.F00
F539 SER.F01
F554 SER.F03
F568 SER.F05
F589 SER.F04
F7F2 DISK.F00
F83C DISK.F01
F844 DISK.F02
F868 DISK.F03
F895 DISK.F06
F89D DISK.F07
F8A9 DISK.F04
F8B8 DISK.F05
F8C7 DISK.F08
